 (exactly) \u0926\u094b \u092c\u093e\u0930 \u0906\u0924\u093e \u0939\u0948, \u0915\u0940 \u0938\u0902\u0916\u094d\u092f\u093e _________ \u0939\u0948\u0964", "options": [], "correct_options": [], "answer": "762", "explanation": null}}, "subject": "mathematics", "timeAllotted": 240000, "topic": "conditional-permutations", "type": "integer", "year": 2025, "yearKey": "jee-advanced-2025-paper-1-online", "active": true, "options": [], "correct_options": ["762"], "explanation": "<p>We form 7-digit numbers using digits $\\{0, 1, 2\\}$. The first digit cannot be $0$, so the first digit must be chosen from $\\{1, 2\\}$.<br>We need the number of elements in $S$ where at least one of $0$ or $1$ appears exactly twice.<br>Let $A$ be the event that digit $0$ appears exactly twice, and $B$ be the event that digit $1$ appears exactly twice.<br>By the principle of inclusion-exclusion:<br>$$|A \\cup B| = |A| + |B| - |A \\cap B|$$<br>\u2022 <b>For |A| (0 appears twice):</b> The first digit can be 1 or 2 (2 choices). The 2 zeros can be placed in any of the remaining 6 places in $\\binom{6}{2} = 15$ ways. The remaining 4 places can be filled with $\\{1, 2\\}$ in $2^4 = 16$ ways: $|A| = 2 \\times 15 \\times 16 = 480$.<br>\u2022 <b>For |B| (1 appears twice):</b><br>Case 1: First digit is 1 (1 choice). Then exactly one more 1 among the remaining 6 places: $\\binom{6}{1} = 6$ ways. The other 5 places are chosen from $\\{0, 2\\}$: $2^5 = 32$ ways $\\implies 1 \\times 6 \\times 32 = 192$.<br>Case 2: First digit is 2 (1 choice). Then two 1s in the remaining 6 places: $\\binom{6}{2} = 15$ ways. The other 4 places from $\\{0, 2\\}$: $2^4 = 16$ ways $\\implies 15 \\times 16 = 240$.<br>Total $|B| = 192 + 240 = 432$.<br>\u2022 <b>For |A &cap; B| (both 0 and 1 appear twice):</b><br>Evaluating the cases yields $|A \\cap B| = 150$.<br>$$|A \\cup B| = 480 + 432 - 150 = 762$$</p>", "qKey": "plet-s-be-the-set-of-all-seven-digit-numbers-that-can-be-jee-advanced-mathematics-z81ein55wkau4ofh", "paperKey": "j